R e a d m e
-----------

Every install measures its own grid, broadcasts it over antenna, and can show
the fleet on any LCD or cockpit screen. There is no separate sender or
dashboard build; which screens a grid shows is only configuration.

SETUP
-----------

1. Put the script on a PB on each ship and base. It creates a [fleet] section
   in the PB's Custom Data with defaults that work as-is.
2. Make sure the grid has a working antenna. Fleet visibility is limited to
   antenna range and relay chains; a grid out of range simply does not appear.
3. Add [fleet] to the name of an LCD or cockpit. Its display_0 is set to
   onboard. Add a key per screen you want to use:
   [fleet]
   display_0=map heading
   display_1=fleet
   display_2=track Miner 1
   The PB's own screens take the same keys in the PB section. Run 'config' to
   list which display_<n> keys each block has screens for.

If the ship has one connector, it is the dock port. With several, mark the
dock port by adding [fleet] to its name or this to its Custom Data:
[fleet]
dock_port=true
Until one is marked the grid reports FLIGHT. A static grid never docks, so it
needs no dock port and never asks for one.

Grids are identified by their grid name (Info tab). Renaming a grid orphans
any track target pointed at the old name.

SCREENS
-----------

onboard              This grid: state lamp, a speed/heading/altitude/crew
                     readout line with a compass tape, then health, power,
                     hydrogen, oxygen and cargo bars. Needs no antenna. A
                     static grid shows neither the motion readouts nor the
                     compass, since it has no speed, facing or height.
fleet                Table of every grid heard: name, state, health, power,
                     H2, O2, cargo, speed, link. Numbers below their warning
                     level turn amber, then red. Stale rows are greyed.
map [north|heading]  Self-centred plot of every contact with its callsign. A
                     contact that reported a heading draws as an arrow facing
                     that way; one that did not, such as a station, draws as a
                     dot. north (default) keeps north up. heading rotates the
                     map to the cockpit's nose. The range auto-fits the
                     farthest contact, rounded up to an even number, and is
                     shown on the ring labels and the scale bar. A screen with
                     room to spare also gets quarter rings, bearing marks and
                     a range under each contact.
track <grid name>    Onboard layout for one other grid from its last
                     broadcast, plus last GPS and link state.

North is world -Z. In natural gravity the map and heading use the horizontal
plane; in space north-up uses the world horizontal plane and heading-up uses
the ship's own deck plane. Contacts broadcast a bearing rather than a
direction, so their map arrows are exact in space and close enough on a
planet for grids near enough to hear each other.

LINK
-----------

The fleet table and the track screen show how the link stands rather than a
stopwatch that reads 0s almost always:

LIVE          Heard from within stale_seconds.
12s, 3m       Time since the last broadcast. Amber once broadcasts start
              being missed, red from three quarters of drop_seconds, when
              the contact is about to fall off the roster.

PB CONFIGURATION
-----------

callsign=          Short map label; blank uses the first four characters of the
                   grid name. Shown in upper case. Keep it short, labels are
                   always visible.
channel=FleetTelemetry   Broadcast tag. Every grid must share it. Anyone with
                   an antenna on the same tag can read the fleet's data.
stale_seconds=8    Contact greyed after this long without a broadcast.
drop_seconds=60    Contact removed after this long without a broadcast.
health_warning_percent=90    Health bar turns red below this.
power_warning_percent=40     Power bar turns red below this.
hydrogen_warning_percent=40  Hydrogen bar turns red below this.
oxygen_warning_percent=40    Oxygen bar turns red below this.
                   Each turns amber in the first third of the room above its
                   level, so 40 warns below 58. Cargo has no level: a full
                   hold is a result, not a fault.
high_altitude_metres=5000    Above this height above ground, altitude is
                   reported above sea level instead and labelled ASL.
show_title=true    Whether this block's screens carry the script title line.
cockpit=           Which cockpit or remote control supplies speed, heading,
                   altitude, position and the map's forward direction. Blank
                   uses the main cockpit, else the first one found. Only set
                   it on a grid with several controllers facing different
                   ways. Use the block's current name.
display_0= display_1=    Roles for the PB's own screens. Add the ones you
                   want; they are not written for you.

Settings with a default are written into Custom Data on first run. Settings
that only matter when you set them, such as callsign, cockpit and unused
display_<n> keys, stay absent until you add them. Missing keys are restored,
invalid values fall back with a warning, and malformed INI is left untouched
while defaults are used. Edits are picked up within about ten seconds or on
any command. Other sections in the same Custom Data, such as [general] from
Tagger or [launchcontrol], are preserved.

WHAT IS MEASURED
-----------

State     STATION for a static grid; DOCKED when the dock port is locked
          (Connected), showing the other grid's name when known; else FLIGHT.
Health    Functional terminal blocks as a share of all terminal blocks.
Power     Batteries stored / capacity.
Hydrogen  Hydrogen tanks. Oxygen: all other gas tanks.
Cargo     Volume used across every block inventory except gas tanks.
Speed, heading, altitude   From the cockpit, and only on a grid that can move.
          Heading is degrees clockwise from north. Altitude is height above
          the ground below (AGL), the same figure the HUD shows; past
          high_altitude_metres it switches to height above sea level (ASL).
          Both need natural gravity.
Crew      Cockpits and remote controls with someone in them.
A dash means the grid has none of that equipment or cannot measure it.

The script runs at Update100 and broadcasts once per run. Contacts are held
in memory only and are cleared by recompile or world reload.

Grids running different versions of this script cannot read each other's
broadcasts: a message that does not match is counted under Rejected and the
grid never appears. Update every PB in the fleet together.

WITH LAUNCHCONTROL
-----------

LaunchControl leaves antennas alone, so docked ships keep broadcasting. Both
scripts use the same dock port rule, so mark the same connector for both if
the ship has several, and measure fullness with the same shared code, so the
two never disagree about a number. Neither reads the other's state. Do not
point both scripts at the same screen; a cockpit can give one screen to each.

COMMANDS
-----------

status     Print own telemetry, contacts and warnings. Default.
config     Print the active settings and which screens each block has.
reload     Reload configuration and rediscover blocks and screens.
clear      Forget every contact.
help       List commands.
